WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.879
We have had lots and lots of requests to
make videos about thorium. People get

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:11.490
really excited about Thorium. We delayed
making the video until we had a sample

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:19.350
of thorium and a friend, Max Whitby, who
makes nice samples of elements, has told

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:25.619
us, on many occasions, that thorium is the
hardest element to get hold of.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:30.119
One of the problems with thorium is simply
obtaining it and there are now very, very

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:32.189
few places in the world where you can
buy any.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:35.800
Even laboratory suppliers don't
sell it.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.390
Finally just before Christmas he, gave us a sample.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.720
Brady - What's that, you got it?

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:47.540
And here is the sample. 
This is a sample of two bits of thorium wire.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:51.960
Now you can see the first problem with
thorium is that it's radioactive,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.649
so you can't post it. I have no idea how
this sample got to London. It was brought

00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:04.769
to me in London in Max's bag, and I
brought it to Nottingham in my pocket.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.470
Now there's no danger bringing it in my
pocket because it's got a very thick

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:16.770
piece of plastic and thorium gives out
relatively weak radioactivity,

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:23.460
so there is no radioactivity outside
this. Thorium as an element is not very

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:33.810
widely used. It used to be used for the
elements in gas fires. To get a good fire

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:38.610
you have some sort of ceramic element
which gets hot and radiates the heat

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:45.350
because flames don't radiate heat very
much, and thorium oxide is very stable so

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.020
people use ceramics based on thorium
oxide.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:53.460
But the reason that people get excited
about thorium is because of nuclear

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:59.850
reactors. Nuclear reactors that are most
widely used in the world are based on

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:04.200
uranium. You remember that different
radioactive elements

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:09.840
can have so-called isotopes. These are
chemically identical atoms but which

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.790
have different numbers of neutrons, and
the uranium isotope which is used in

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:21.599
nuclear reactors is uranium 235, which
when it is hit by neutrons can split

00:02:21.599 --> 00:02:27.239
giving out more neutrons and you get to
chain reaction. In a nuclear reactor you

00:02:27.239 --> 00:02:32.580
want the reaction to go nice and slowly
to keep the thing hot so you can turn

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.549
water into steam and generate
electricity.

00:02:35.549 --> 00:02:40.590
(V.O.) Thus we obtain a constantly increasing
number of fissions called a chain reaction.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:45.150
Prof. Poliakoff - If you're building an atom bomb -- 
a nuclear weapon --

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:49.260
you want all your uranium to go bang at
the same moment.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.670
The problem with current nuclear
technology is first of all that

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:02.640
potentially there's a shortage of
uranium.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.970
It's a very rare element. Equally
important

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:13.410
you can use the uranium in the reactor.
The uranium 238, which is the isotope

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:18.180
that doesn't take part in the reaction,
can be used to absorb neutrons and make

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.200
plutonium, which can then be used in
nuclear weapons.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:28.230
So if you build a reactor somewhere,
there is a danger that the waste

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:34.620
products from that reactor (or some of the waste products) could be used for making nuclear bombs.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:41.840
The atoms of the metals uranium and plutonium can be most readily fissioned to cause nuclear explosion.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:48.260
And everybody in the world generally agrees that we have to try and reduce the number of bombs rather than

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:53.220
making it easier for people to make more
bombs. Thorium can be used for a

00:03:53.220 --> 00:04:00.800
completely different approach to nuclear
reactors, but which still involves uranium.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:06.440
The most abundant isotope of
thorium is thorium 232.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:14.700
If this isotope absorbs a neutron it
becomes thorium 233; it's mass goes up by one.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:22.290
Then sequentially, two of the neutrons
split into a proton and an electron and

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:27.419
so it goes... jumps from thorium to protactinium to
uranium,

00:04:27.419 --> 00:04:37.080
so you generate uranium-233. Uranium-233
is a very rare isotope of uranium but

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:42.930
you can breed it from the thorium which
you've got a lot of, and the uranium-233

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:47.400
can then do your nuclear reaction, break
apart,

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:51.150
give you energy, just like the other
uranium isotopes.

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:56.610
But the difference is that it can't
produce plutonium and therefore it

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.930
cannot be used to make bombs

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:02.680
Of course you can say, well, if it splits
in the nuclear reactor,

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:09.789
why can't you make a bomb out of
uranium-233? And the answer is, in

00:05:09.789 --> 00:05:15.370
principle you could, but as a by-product
of all the nuclear processes

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:23.979
you also get uranium 232 and uranium 232
is a showstopper. It absorbs neutrons and

00:05:23.979 --> 00:05:28.090
stops uranium exploding like a bomb,

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:33.880
so it's safe in that way. The other
advantage of thorium is that it's more

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:41.229
abundant in the earth, and because
thorium-232 is the principal isotope

00:05:41.229 --> 00:05:45.529
compared to uranium 235, which is in only
a small amount

00:05:45.529 --> 00:05:52.940
there is an awful lot more thorium that could be used in reactors in the world than there is uranium.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:57.880
(V.O.) - ...except you use a nuclear reactor to produce phenomenal quantities of heat.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:04.120
Prof. Poliakoff - The disadvantage is that you need to have some neutrons to get the thing started.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:10.270
You have to have a nuclear reaction to
produce neutrons so that your thorium

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:16.120
will turn into uranium. But that also
can be quite an advantage, because for

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:22.090
example you can put waste plutonium
mixed with the thorium and you burn up

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:25.150
the plutonium and you get more fuel,

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:31.750
so "breeding reactor". The stability of
thorium oxide, the one that was exploited

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:35.830
years ago in gas fires, is both an
advantage and disadvantage.

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:40.419
It's an advantage because your fuel is
very stable.

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:45.639
The downside of thorium dioxide being so
stable is that you have to heat it to a

00:06:45.639 --> 00:06:52.449
very high temperature to process it, and
that's not easy. Relatively little work

00:06:52.449 --> 00:06:59.500
has been done on thorium reactors,
compared to the now 70 years or more,

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:05.680
nearly 80 years, of research that have
been done on uranium/plutonium reactors.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.000
And therefore, the engineering, the safety
and so on

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:15.550
is not developed to the same level and the
costs of building full-size thorium

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:18.210
reactors would be very considerable.

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:20.320
Brady - It sounds like if all things being equal

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:24.280
and we could start again, you would
advocate going down the thorium route

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:28.900
and we are only doing the uranium/plutonium route because we've been doing it for so long.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:37.990
Well you have to remember that uranium/plutonium route started because of the

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:41.770
Manhattan Project. The atomic bomb project.

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:50.710
I suspect that if nuclear power had been
just started for civilian use, that the

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:55.510
cost would have been prohibitive and
people might never have used nuclear

00:07:55.510 --> 00:08:03.970
power, so it is built on the legacy of
the weapons programs in many of the

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.130
major countries in the world.

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:11.710
We now have the situation where we
understand how to handle radioactive

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:17.680
compounds. We don't understand entirely
how to deal with the waste in practical

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:23.560
terms that we do in principle, so it
would be much more feasible now to go on

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:27.220
the thorium cycle than it would have been in the 1940s.

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:36.900
Brady - If you'd like to see a bit more footage
from this interview check out the links

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:40.979
on the screen or the video description. I'll
also include links to more videos about

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:46.400
uranium, radioactive elements, and our
trip to the nuclear facility at Sellafield.

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:49.880
Chris Maher - Yeah, they're
centered uranium dioxide pellets

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:53.050
suitable for radiation and nuclear
reactors.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:57.320
Brady - And if you'd like to support periodic videos -- maybe sponsor an element or just help us out --

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:03.360
you can check out our Patreon page. That's patreon.com/periodicvideos

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:12.840
(V.O.) - The sure hand of science brings power unlimited
from the magic of the atom

